    │   ├── src/app.css          # Tailwind source
    │   └── dist/app.css         # Tailwind output (gitignored)
    └── static.go                # //go:embed for static files
```

## How HTMX talks to Go

Wails v3's `AssetServer` accepts any `http.Handler`. We mount a `ServeMux`:

```go
mux.Handle("POST /api/counter/inc", http.HandlerFunc(counterInc))
```

The handler renders a Templ component directly to the response writer:

```go
components.Counter(cur+1).Render(r.Context(), w)
```

HTMX swaps the returned HTML into the page. No JSON, no Wails bindings, no localhost — Wails routes everything through its in-process asset server.

## Tests

Handler tests use `httptest.NewRecorder` and assert on rendered HTML substrings — examples in `internal/handlers/*_test.go` and `internal/server/middleware_test.go`.

```bash
go test ./internal/...
```

## Build & ship

**Always run `task build` — never `wails3 build` directly.** Wails v3 does not support pre-build hooks, so the Taskfile is what guarantees `templ generate` and the Tailwind build run before the Wails binary is compiled. Running `wails3 build` on its own will produce a binary with stale `*_templ.go` files and a missing `web/static/dist/app.css`.

```bash
task build        # generates Templ + Tailwind, then builds Wails binary into ./bin/
./bin/{{.ProjectName}}
```

The binary is single-file: `web/static/` is embedded with `//go:embed`.

